IMPORT prelude
IMPORT `fcfca-dates`

-- Federal Circuit and Family Court of Australia Act 2021 (Cth) -- baseline encoding from the seat of the person bringing
-- (or answering) a civil claim in the Federal Circuit and Family Court of Australia (Division 2), and appealing from it.
--
-- SEAT. The party to the proceeding: usually the applicant who brings a claim for money, damages, a declaration or other
-- relief (a worker's Fair Work claim, a discrimination claim, a debt or a Commonwealth tenancy dispute), and the same
-- person as appellant, as the subject of a suppression or vexatious proceedings application, or as the party affected by
-- a Registrar's decision. Questions are phrased as that person asks them. The Court, its Judges, the Chief Judge, the
-- Chief Justice, Registrars and the Minister get no seat: their satisfactions, opinions, directions and orders are asked
-- as facts.
--
-- Every question takes plain facts (yes/no, a number, a date, a choice from a list). No question takes a DEONTIC or an
-- EVENT. The two duties with a deadline (s 215 security for costs; s 256 review of a delegate's decision) are regulative
-- rules run inside a question.
--
-- PROVISIONS COVERED (Chapter 4, the Federal Circuit and Family Court of Australia (Division 2), and the appeal routes in
-- Chapter 3): s 7 (definitions used: Commonwealth tenancy dispute, family law or child support proceedings, judgment,
-- non-publication order, suppression order, vexatious proceeding, vexatious proceedings order); s 26(1)(c), (h), (2)(b),
-- s 28(1), s 50, s 55(1)-(3), (6); ss 131(3), 132 (as the family law test), 133(1), 135, 136(1), (2), (2A), (3), (4), (7),
-- 137(1), 143, 149(4), 153(6), 155, 156, 161, 168, 169, 170(1), (3), 172, 173(4), 176, 185, 190(1), 191, 191A,
-- 192(3)-(4), 211, 212, 213, 214, 215, 228-234, 236, 237, 239, 241-244, 256(1)-(2). What is out, and why, is in NOTES.md.
--
-- Edition: Federal Circuit and Family Court of Australia Act 2021, Compilation No. 12, compilation date 10 June 2025
-- (register id C2025C00376), deposited at ../../registers/source-bundle/C2021A00012.txt. The Rules of Court and the
-- regulations that set the claimant's numbers (the judgment interest rate, s 212(3)(a); the time to seek review of a
-- delegate's decision, s 256(1)(a); the "prescribed judgment" of s 28(1)(b)) are not deposited, so each is an input.
--
-- Sourced from the Federal Register of Legislation, Commonwealth of Australia, CC BY 4.0 (SOURCE-LICENSE.md).
-- BASELINE ENCODING. Not for public use as advice. Judgments the Act hands to the Court are INPUTS ("if the Court is
-- satisfied that ..."). Answers follow the rulings on negatives of 2026-11-04 and 2026-11-05 (NOTES.md): an answer that
-- is the text's own no is marked "(the text's no)"; an answer where the text is silent or limited by its own wording is
-- marked "NOTHING-kind" and worded "does not reach it".

§ `Where my claim is heard, and how`

-- s 7: "family law or child support proceedings means proceedings in respect of which the Federal Circuit and Family
-- Court of Australia (Division 2) has original jurisdiction under section 132" (the Family Law Act 1975, the Marriage Act
-- 1961, certain Territory laws, and the two child support Acts). The kind of proceeding is asked as a fact.
DECLARE `My kind of proceeding` IS ONE OF
    `a family law proceeding (under the Family Law Act 1975, the Marriage Act 1961 or a Territory family law; s 132(1)(a)-(c))`
    `a child support proceeding (under the Child Support (Assessment) Act 1989 or the Child Support (Registration and Collection) Act 1988; s 132(1)(d))`
    `another civil proceeding (general federal law, Fair Work, discrimination, a Commonwealth tenancy dispute or other)`

GIVEN k IS A `My kind of proceeding`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `s 7 -- a family law or child support proceeding` k IF
    NOT (k EQUALS `another civil proceeding (general federal law, Fair Work, discrimination, a Commonwealth tenancy dispute or other)`)

-- "proceedings under the Family Law Act 1975" (ss 156, 191A, 228, 239(3)) are narrower than "family law or child support
-- proceedings": a child support proceeding is not one of them (s 156 note 2).
GIVEN k IS A `My kind of proceeding`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `a proceeding under the Family Law Act 1975` k IF
    k EQUALS `a family law proceeding (under the Family Law Act 1975, the Marriage Act 1961 or a Territory family law; s 132(1)(a)-(c))`

-- s 135(2): "Every proceeding in the Federal Circuit and Family Court of Australia (Division 2) must be instituted, heard
-- and determined in a Division mentioned in subsection (1)." (3): the Fair Work Division takes "jurisdiction that is
-- required by any other Act to be exercised in the Fair Work Division" and what is incidental to it (note: Fair Work Act
-- 2009 s 567). (4): the General Division takes the rest. (5): where jurisdiction "is required to be exercised in both",
-- the Chief Judge "may ... give a direction about the allocation".
DECLARE `Which Division of Division 2` IS ONE OF
    `the Fair Work Division (s 135(3))`
    `the General Division (s 135(4))`
    `the Division the Chief Judge directs; until a direction, the Act does not say (s 135(5))`

@export Which Division of the Federal Circuit and Family Court of Australia (Division 2) must my claim be brought in (Federal Circuit and Family Court of Australia Act 2021 (Cth) s 135)? Every proceeding is brought in the General Division or the Fair Work Division. Claims another Act sends to the Fair Work Division (for example matters arising under the Fair Work Act 2009) go there; all others go to the General Division.
GIVEN `required in the Fair Work Division` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether another Act requires your claim to be heard in the Fair Work Division (for example a claim arising under the Fair Work Act 2009, which s 567 of that Act sends there)
      `also required in the General Division` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether some other part of your claim is required by another Act to be heard in the General Division as well
GIVETH A `Which Division of Division 2`
`s 135 -- which Division my claim goes to` MEANS
    IF `required in the Fair Work Division` AND `also required in the General Division`
    THEN `the Division the Chief Judge directs; until a direction, the Act does not say (s 135(5))`
    ELSE IF `required in the Fair Work Division`
         THEN `the Fair Work Division (s 135(3))`
         ELSE `the General Division (s 135(4))`

-- s 136(1): "This section does not apply to family law or child support proceedings." (2): "must be exercised in open
-- court", save where a Judge sits in Chambers as authorised. (7): the Court "may order the exclusion of the public ... if
-- the Court is satisfied that the presence of the public ... would be: (a) contrary to the interests of justice; or (b)
-- prejudicial to the security of the Commonwealth." s 137(1): "exercised by the Court constituted by a single Judge".
-- s 185: "A civil proceeding between parties ... is to be determined without a jury."
@export How will my claim in the Federal Circuit and Family Court of Australia (Division 2) be heard (Federal Circuit and Family Court of Australia Act 2021 (Cth) ss 136, 137, 185)? By a single Judge, without a jury, and (outside family law and child support) in open court unless the Judge sits in Chambers as the Act or the Rules allow, or the Court excludes the public because their presence would be contrary to the interests of justice or prejudicial to the security of the Commonwealth.
GIVEN `my kind of proceeding` IS A `My kind of proceeding` @desc What your proceeding is about
      `the public excluded` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Court has ordered the public (or particular people) excluded, being satisfied their presence would be contrary to the interests of justice or prejudicial to the security of the Commonwealth
      `in Chambers` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the step is one the Act or the Rules let a Judge deal with in Chambers (for example an application about the conduct of the proceeding, or a decision without an oral hearing that the parties consented to)
GIVETH A LIST OF STRING
`ss 136, 137, 185 -- how my claim is heard` MEANS
    LIST
        "by a single Judge (s 137(1))",
        "without a jury (s 185)",
        `where`
    WHERE
        `where` MEANS
            IF `s 7 -- a family law or child support proceeding` `my kind of proceeding`
            THEN "open court or not: s 136 does not apply; the Family Law Act 1975 s 97 governs (s 136(1))"
            ELSE IF `the public excluded`
                 THEN "in court with the public, or the persons named, excluded by order (s 136(7))"
                 ELSE IF `in Chambers`
                      THEN "by a Judge sitting in Chambers (s 136(3), (4))"
                      ELSE "in open court, which includes a hearing made public by video link, audio link or other means (s 136(2), (2A))"

-- s 133(1): Division 2 "has original jurisdiction to hear and determine a Commonwealth tenancy dispute between the parties
-- to a lease, licence or other arrangement in which: (a) the Commonwealth ... is: (i) the lessor (other than as a
-- sublessor); or (ii) the licensor (other than as a sublicensor); or (iii) the grantor of a right or permission to
-- possess, occupy or use land owned by the Commonwealth; and (b) a person other than: (i) the Commonwealth; ... (iii) a
-- Commonwealth officer or employee; is: (iv) the lessee (other than as a sublessee); or (v) the licensee (other than as
-- a sublicensee); or (vi) the grantee of the right or permission." s 7 "Commonwealth tenancy dispute": a dispute about
-- (i) rent or other payments, (ii) termination, or (iii) possession, occupation or use, in which the Commonwealth is a
-- party. s 133(2): the Minister may confer jurisdiction over "any other specified Commonwealth tenancy dispute" by
-- legislative instrument (none deposited).
DECLARE `The Commonwealth's part in my arrangement` IS ONE OF
    `the Commonwealth is my lessor, licensor or grantor directly (not a sublessor or sublicensor)`
    `the Commonwealth is a sublessor or sublicensor, or not my landlord at all`

DECLARE `My part in the arrangement` IS ONE OF
    `I am the lessee, licensee or grantee directly (not a sublessee or sublicensee)`
    `I am a sublessee or sublicensee`

DECLARE `What the dispute is about` IS ONE OF
    `rent or other payments under the arrangement`
    `ending the arrangement`
    `possession, occupation or use of the land`
    `something else`

@export Can I bring my dispute with the Commonwealth over a lease or licence of land in the Federal Circuit and Family Court of Australia (Division 2) (Federal Circuit and Family Court of Australia Act 2021 (Cth) ss 7, 133)? Yes, where the Commonwealth is directly your lessor, licensor or grantor, you are directly the lessee, licensee or grantee and not a Commonwealth officer or employee, and the dispute is about rent or payments, ending the arrangement, or possession, occupation or use of the land. Other disputes are reached only if the Minister has conferred jurisdiction by legislative instrument.
GIVEN `the Commonwealth's part` IS A `The Commonwealth's part in my arrangement` @desc How the Commonwealth stands in your lease, licence or permission to use land
      `my part` IS A `My part in the arrangement` @desc How you stand in it
      `I am a Commonwealth officer or employee` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are a Commonwealth officer or employee
      `the land is Commonwealth land` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the land is owned by the Commonwealth (needed only where your arrangement is a right or permission, not a lease or licence)
      `a lease or licence` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your arrangement is a lease or a licence (rather than another right or permission to use land)
      `the dispute` IS A `What the dispute is about` @desc What the dispute is about
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 133 -- can I bring my Commonwealth tenancy dispute in Division 2` MEANS
    IF     `the Commonwealth's part` EQUALS `the Commonwealth is my lessor, licensor or grantor directly (not a sublessor or sublicensor)`
       AND `my part` EQUALS `I am the lessee, licensee or grantee directly (not a sublessee or sublicensee)`
       AND (NOT `I am a Commonwealth officer or employee`)
       AND (`a lease or licence` OR `the land is Commonwealth land`)
       AND (NOT (`the dispute` EQUALS `something else`))
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE NOTHING -- NOTHING-kind: s 133(1) is a grant limited by its own wording, and s 133(2) lets the Minister confer more (no instrument deposited)

§ `Moving the claim along: summary judgment, discovery, directions, costs of conduct`

-- s 143(1): judgment for the party "prosecuting the proceeding" if "the Court is satisfied that the other party has no
-- reasonable prospect of successfully defending"; (2): judgment for the party "defending" if satisfied "the other party
-- has no reasonable prospect of successfully prosecuting". (3): it "need not be: (a) hopeless; or (b) bound to fail".
-- (5): "This section does not apply if the ... Court is exercising jurisdiction under the Family Law Act 1975."
DECLARE `My side of the proceeding` IS ONE OF
    `I am bringing the claim`
    `I am defending the claim`

DECLARE `What s 143 means for me` IS ONE OF
    `the Court may give judgment for me now, on the whole or that part (s 143)`
    `the Court may give judgment against me now, on the whole or that part (s 143)`
    `s 143 does not apply: Family Law Act jurisdiction (s 143(5); see Family Law Act s 102QAB)`
    `s 143 does not reach it on these facts; the case goes on`

@export Can my claim, or the other side's, be decided early by summary judgment in the Federal Circuit and Family Court of Australia (Division 2) (Federal Circuit and Family Court of Australia Act 2021 (Cth) s 143)? The Court may give judgment for one party on the whole or part of a proceeding if it is satisfied the other party has no reasonable prospect of success; the case need not be hopeless or bound to fail. Not in Family Law Act matters.
GIVEN `my kind of proceeding` IS A `My kind of proceeding` @desc What your proceeding is about
      `my side` IS A `My side of the proceeding` @desc Whether you are bringing the claim (or that part of it) or defending it
      `the other side has no reasonable prospect` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Court is satisfied the other party has no reasonable prospect of success on the whole or that part
      `I have no reasonable prospect` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Court is satisfied you have no reasonable prospect of success on the whole or that part
GIVETH A `What s 143 means for me`
`s 143 -- summary judgment` MEANS
    IF `a proceeding under the Family Law Act 1975` `my kind of proceeding`
    THEN `s 143 does not apply: Family Law Act jurisdiction (s 143(5); see Family Law Act s 102QAB)` -- the text's no
    ELSE IF `the other side has no reasonable prospect`
         THEN `the Court may give judgment for me now, on the whole or that part (s 143)`
         ELSE IF `I have no reasonable prospect`
              THEN `the Court may give judgment against me now, on the whole or that part (s 143)`
              ELSE `s 143 does not reach it on these facts; the case goes on` -- NOTHING-kind: s 143(4) keeps the Court's other powers

-- s 176(1): "Interrogatories and discovery are allowed in relation to family law and child support proceedings". (2):
-- "not allowed in relation to any other proceedings ... unless the Court or a Judge declares that it is appropriate, in
-- the interests of the administration of justice". The declaration is the Court's: an input.
@export Can I get discovery of documents or serve interrogatories on the other side in the Federal Circuit and Family Court of Australia (Division 2) (Federal Circuit and Family Court of Australia Act 2021 (Cth) s 176)? In family law and child support proceedings, yes. In other proceedings, only if the Court or a Judge declares it appropriate in the interests of the administration of justice.
GIVEN `my kind of proceeding` IS A `My kind of proceeding` @desc What your proceeding is about
      `the Court has declared it appropriate` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Court or a Judge has declared that interrogatories or discovery are appropriate in the interests of the administration of justice
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `s 176 -- can I have discovery or interrogatories` IF
       `s 7 -- a family law or child support proceeding` `my kind of proceeding`
    OR `the Court has declared it appropriate` -- otherwise the text's no ("not allowed ... unless")

-- s 191(1): the parties "must conduct the proceeding (including negotiations for settlement ...) in a way that is
-- consistent with the overarching purpose" (s 190(1): "the just resolution of disputes: (a) according to law; and (b) as
-- quickly, inexpensively and efficiently as possible"). (2): the lawyer must assist. (3): the Court may require the lawyer
-- to give an estimate of duration and costs. (4): "In exercising the discretion to award costs ... must take account of
-- any failure to comply with the duty imposed by subsection (1) or (2)." (5): "may order a party's lawyer to bear costs
-- personally". (6): then "the lawyer must not recover the costs from the lawyer's client." s 191A: ss 190 and 191 "do not
-- apply in relation to proceedings under the Family Law Act 1975". s 192(3), (4): on failure to comply with a direction,
-- the Court may "(a) dismiss the proceeding in whole or in part; (b) strike out, amend or limit any part of a party's claim
-- or defence; (c) disallow or reject any evidence; (d) award costs against a party; (e) order that costs ... be assessed
-- on an indemnity basis or otherwise."
@export What can follow if I, or my lawyer, do not conduct my claim in the Federal Circuit and Family Court of Australia (Division 2) as the Act requires (Federal Circuit and Family Court of Australia Act 2021 (Cth) ss 191, 191A, 192)? A failure to act consistently with the overarching purpose (just resolution, as quickly, inexpensively and efficiently as possible) must be taken into account in costs, and a lawyer can be made to bear costs personally without recovering them from you. Failing to follow a direction can lead to dismissal, striking out, evidence being rejected or costs, including indemnity costs.
GIVEN `my kind of proceeding` IS A `My kind of proceeding` @desc What your proceeding is about
      `I failed the overarching purpose duty` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Court finds you did not conduct the proceeding (including settlement negotiations) consistently with the overarching purpose
      `my lawyer failed the duty` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Court finds your lawyer did not take account of your duty or assist you to comply with it
      `I failed to follow a direction` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Court finds you did not comply with a direction it gave about the practice and procedure of the proceeding
GIVETH A LIST OF STRING
`ss 191, 192 -- what can follow from how my claim is conducted` MEANS
    append `the s 191 consequences` `the s 192 consequences`
    WHERE
        `s 191 applies` MEANS NOT (`a proceeding under the Family Law Act 1975` `my kind of proceeding`)
        `the s 191 consequences` MEANS
            IF `s 191 applies` AND (`I failed the overarching purpose duty` OR `my lawyer failed the duty`)
            THEN LIST
                "the Court must take the failure into account in deciding costs (s 191(4))",
                "the Court may order your lawyer to bear costs personally (s 191(5))",
                `the s 191(6) line`
            ELSE EMPTY
        `the s 191(6) line` MEANS
            IF `my lawyer failed the duty`
            THEN "if your lawyer is ordered to bear costs personally for their own failure, they must not recover those costs from you (s 191(6))"
            ELSE "an order on your lawyer for your own failure is in the Court's discretion (s 191(5))"
        `the s 192 consequences` MEANS
            IF `I failed to follow a direction`
            THEN LIST
                "the proceeding may be dismissed in whole or in part (s 192(4)(a))",
                "any part of your claim or defence may be struck out, amended or limited (s 192(4)(b))",
                "evidence may be disallowed or rejected (s 192(4)(c))",
                "costs may be awarded against you (s 192(4)(d))",
                "costs against you may be ordered on an indemnity basis or otherwise (s 192(4)(e))"
            ELSE EMPTY

§ `Mediation, conciliation and arbitration`

-- s 156: Part 5 does not apply to proceedings under the Family Law Act 1975 (it does apply to child support, but
-- Division 2 of Part 5 does not: s 168). s 161(1), (3): conciliation, "with or without the consent of the parties".
-- s 169(1), (3): mediation, "with or without the consent"; (4): what is said or admitted at the mediation conference "is
-- not admissible" in any court or before any person authorised to hear evidence. s 170(1), (3): arbitration "only with the
-- consent of the parties".
DECLARE `The dispute resolution process` IS ONE OF
    `conciliation (s 161)`
    `mediation (s 169)`
    `arbitration (s 170)`

DECLARE `Can I be referred` IS ONE OF
    `yes, even without my consent`
    `yes, but only with my consent (and the other parties')`
    `no: I have not consented, and arbitration needs the consent of the parties (s 170(3))`
    `not under these sections: the Family Law Act 1975 governs (s 156)`
    `not under these sections: child support proceedings are outside Division 2 of Part 5 (s 168)`

@export Can the Federal Circuit and Family Court of Australia (Division 2) send my claim to conciliation, mediation or arbitration, and do I have to agree (Federal Circuit and Family Court of Australia Act 2021 (Cth) ss 156, 161, 168, 169, 170)? Conciliation and mediation can be ordered with or without your consent; arbitration only with the consent of the parties. Mediation and arbitration referrals do not apply to family law or child support proceedings, and this Part does not apply at all to Family Law Act proceedings.
GIVEN `my kind of proceeding` IS A `My kind of proceeding` @desc What your proceeding is about
      `the process` IS A `The dispute resolution process` @desc The process the Court would refer the proceeding (or part of it) to
      `the parties consent` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you and the other parties consent to the referral
GIVETH A `Can I be referred`
`ss 161, 169, 170 -- can I be referred` MEANS
    IF `a proceeding under the Family Law Act 1975` `my kind of proceeding`
    THEN `not under these sections: the Family Law Act 1975 governs (s 156)` -- the text's no: "This Part applies to proceedings ... other than"
    ELSE IF `the process` EQUALS `conciliation (s 161)`
         THEN `yes, even without my consent`
         ELSE IF `s 7 -- a family law or child support proceeding` `my kind of proceeding`
              THEN `not under these sections: child support proceedings are outside Division 2 of Part 5 (s 168)` -- the text's no
              ELSE IF `the process` EQUALS `mediation (s 169)`
                   THEN `yes, even without my consent`
                   ELSE IF `the parties consent`
                        THEN `yes, but only with my consent (and the other parties')`
                        ELSE `no: I have not consented, and arbitration needs the consent of the parties (s 170(3))` -- the text's no: "only with the consent"

@export Can what I say or admit at a court-ordered mediation be used against me in evidence (Federal Circuit and Family Court of Australia Act 2021 (Cth) s 169(4))? No: evidence of anything said, or any admission made, at a conference conducted by a mediator on a referral by the Federal Circuit and Family Court of Australia (Division 2) is not admissible in any court or before anyone authorised to hear evidence.
GIVEN `referred by the Court under s 169` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the mediation was one the Court referred the proceeding (or part of it) to under s 169
      `said at the mediator's conference` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the statement or admission was made at a conference conducted by the mediator
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 169(4) -- can my words at mediation be used in evidence` MEANS
    IF `referred by the Court under s 169` AND `said at the mediator's conference`
    THEN JUST FALSE -- the text's no: "is not admissible"
    ELSE NOTHING -- NOTHING-kind: s 169(4) is limited by its own wording to conferences on a s 169 referral

-- s 172(1): where proceedings were referred under s 170(1) to arbitration, "the arbitrator has made an award" and "the
-- award has been registered with the Court under the Rules of Court": (2) "A party to the award may apply ... for a
-- review, on a question of law, of the award." (3): the Court may determine the question and affirm, vary, set aside and
-- remit, or set aside and determine. (4): a party may apply for an order that the costs it must pay for the arbitration
-- "be taxed in accordance with the Rules of Court". (5): "The person who made the application is not liable to pay, in
-- respect of the costs of the arbitration, an amount that is more than the amount of the costs as taxed".
@export Can I have an arbitration award in my claim reviewed by the Federal Circuit and Family Court of Australia (Division 2) (Federal Circuit and Family Court of Australia Act 2021 (Cth) s 172(1)-(3))? Where the Court referred your proceeding to arbitration, an award has been made and it has been registered with the Court, you may apply for review on a question of law. The Court may affirm, vary, or set aside the award (and remit it or decide the matter itself).
GIVEN `referred to arbitration by the Court` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Court referred the proceeding (or part of it) to arbitration under s 170
      `an award made` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the arbitrator has made an award
      `the award registered` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the award has been registered with the Court under the Rules of Court
      `on a question of law` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your complaint about the award is on a question of law (rather than of fact)
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 172 -- can I have the arbitration award reviewed` MEANS
    IF `referred to arbitration by the Court` AND `an award made` AND `the award registered`
    THEN IF `on a question of law`
         THEN JUST TRUE
         ELSE NOTHING -- NOTHING-kind: s 172(2) gives review "on a question of law" and says nothing of other questions
    ELSE JUST FALSE -- the text's no: the s 172(1) "If" conditions are not met

@export What is the most I can be made to pay for the costs of an arbitration the Court sent my claim to, if I have them taxed (Federal Circuit and Family Court of Australia Act 2021 (Cth) s 172(4), (5))? If you applied for, and the Court ordered, taxation of the arbitration costs you must pay, you are not liable for more than the taxed amount. Otherwise the Act sets no limit.
GIVEN `I applied and taxation was ordered` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you applied, and the Court or a Judge ordered, that the costs you must pay for the arbitration be taxed under the Rules of Court (s 172(4))
      `the costs claimed from me` IS A NUMBER @desc The costs of the arbitration claimed from you, in dollars
      `the costs as taxed` IS A NUMBER @desc The amount of those costs as taxed, in dollars
GIVETH A MAYBE NUMBER
`s 172(5) -- the most I pay for the arbitration costs` MEANS
    IF `I applied and taxation was ordered`
    THEN JUST (min `the costs claimed from me` `the costs as taxed`)
    ELSE NOTHING -- NOTHING-kind: without a s 172(4) order the Act sets no limit

§ `Interest, judgment and enforcement`

-- s 211(1): "This section does not apply to family law or child support proceedings." (2): a party to proceedings "for
-- the recovery of any money (including any debt or damages or the value of any goods) in respect of a particular cause of
-- action may apply". (3): if "the Court or the Judge is not satisfied that good cause has been shown for not making an
-- order", it "must either: (c) order that there be included in the sum for which judgment is given interest at such rate
-- as the Court or the Judge thinks fit on the whole or any part of the money for the whole or any part of the period
-- between: (i) the date when the cause of action arose; and (ii) the date as of which judgment is entered; or (d) ...
-- a lump sum in lieu of any such interest." (4): it does not "(a) authorise the giving of interest upon interest ...;
-- (b) apply in relation to any debt upon which interest is payable as of right ...; (e) authorise the giving of interest
-- ... otherwise than by consent, upon any sum for which judgment is given by consent."
DECLARE `What s 211 gives me` IS ONE OF
    `the Court must add interest (or a lump sum instead) to my judgment (s 211(3))`
    `s 211 does not apply: family law or child support (s 211(1))`
    `s 211(3) does not apply: interest is payable on the debt as of right (s 211(4)(b))`
    `no interest under s 211 on a consent judgment unless the parties consent to it (s 211(4)(e))`
    `s 211 does not reach it: not a claim for the recovery of money`
    `s 211 does not reach it: good cause shown against an order`
    `not until I apply for an interest order (s 211(2))`

@export Can I get interest added to my judgment for the time before judgment in the Federal Circuit and Family Court of Australia (Division 2) (Federal Circuit and Family Court of Australia Act 2021 (Cth) s 211)? In a claim to recover money (a debt, damages or the value of goods), if you apply, the Court must add interest at a rate it thinks fit, or a lump sum instead, unless good cause is shown against it. Not in family law or child support proceedings, not where the debt already carries interest as of right, and not on a consent judgment unless the parties consent.
GIVEN `my kind of proceeding` IS A `My kind of proceeding` @desc What your proceeding is about
      `a claim to recover money` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your claim is for the recovery of money, including a debt, damages or the value of goods, on a particular cause of action
      `I have applied for interest` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have applied to the Court for an interest order under s 211(2)
      `interest payable as of right` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the debt already carries interest as of right, for example under your agreement
      `judgment by consent` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether judgment is given by consent
      `interest consented to` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the parties consented to interest on that consent judgment
      `good cause shown against an order` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Court is satisfied that good cause has been shown for not making an interest order
GIVETH A `What s 211 gives me`
`s 211 -- can I get interest up to judgment` MEANS
    IF `s 7 -- a family law or child support proceeding` `my kind of proceeding`
    THEN `s 211 does not apply: family law or child support (s 211(1))` -- the text's no
    ELSE IF (NOT `a claim to recover money`)
         THEN `s 211 does not reach it: not a claim for the recovery of money` -- NOTHING-kind: s 211(4)(d) keeps other interest laws
         ELSE IF `interest payable as of right`
              THEN `s 211(3) does not apply: interest is payable on the debt as of right (s 211(4)(b))` -- the text's no
              ELSE IF `judgment by consent` AND (NOT `interest consented to`)
                   THEN `no interest under s 211 on a consent judgment unless the parties consent to it (s 211(4)(e))` -- the text's no
                   ELSE IF (NOT `I have applied for interest`)
                        THEN `not until I apply for an interest order (s 211(2))` -- the text's no: the s 211(3) "If ... an application is made"
                        ELSE IF `good cause shown against an order`
                             THEN `s 211 does not reach it: good cause shown against an order` -- NOTHING-kind: the "must" falls away; whether the Court may still order is not said (fork F3)
                             ELSE `the Court must add interest (or a lump sum instead) to my judgment (s 211(3))`

-- s 211(5): no interest "in respect of" an amount for "(c) compensation in respect of liabilities incurred which do not
-- carry interest as against the person claiming interest ...; (d) compensation for loss or damage to be incurred or
-- suffered after the date on which judgment is given; or (e) exemplary or punitive damages". (6): interest may still be
-- given "upon compensation in respect of a liability of the kind referred to in paragraph (5)(c), where that liability has
-- been met by the applicant, as from the date upon which that liability was so met". (4)(a): no "interest upon interest":
-- so simple interest. The rate is the Court's ("such rate as the Court or the Judge thinks fit"): an input, a percentage
-- a year. The Act gives no day-count; 365 days a year is this encoding's assumption (fork F4). The Court may choose part
-- of the money and part of the period, or a lump sum instead: this question gives the most the Act allows at that rate.
GIVEN `the money` IS A NUMBER, `rate percent a year` IS A NUMBER, `days` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`simple interest on` `the money` `at` `rate percent a year` `for` `days` MEANS
    (`the money` TIMES `rate percent a year` DIVIDED BY 100) TIMES `days` DIVIDED BY 365

@export What is the most interest the Court can add for the time before judgment, at the rate it fixes (Federal Circuit and Family Court of Australia Act 2021 (Cth) s 211(3)-(6))? Simple interest (no interest on interest) from the day your cause of action arose to the day judgment is entered, on the money awarded except compensation for future loss, exemplary or punitive damages, and compensation for liabilities that do not carry interest; such a liability you have already paid carries interest from the day you paid it. The Court may give less, or a lump sum instead. Uses 365 days a year.
GIVEN `the sum for which judgment is given` IS A NUMBER @desc The total sum awarded in your judgment, in dollars
      `future loss` IS A NUMBER @desc The part of it that is compensation for loss or damage to be suffered after judgment, in dollars
      `exemplary or punitive damages` IS A NUMBER @desc The part of it that is exemplary or punitive damages, in dollars
      `non-interest-bearing liabilities not yet paid` IS A NUMBER @desc The part of it that compensates you for liabilities you incurred that do not carry interest against you and that you have not yet paid, in dollars
      `non-interest-bearing liabilities paid` IS A NUMBER @desc The part of it that compensates you for such liabilities that you have already paid, in dollars
      `liabilities paid on` IS A DATE @desc The day you paid those liabilities (if none, any date; it is not used)
      `cause of action arose on` IS A DATE @desc The day your cause of action arose
      `judgment entered on` IS A DATE @desc The day as of which judgment is (or will be) entered
      `rate percent a year` IS A NUMBER @desc The interest rate the Court fixes, as a percentage a year (for example 6 for 6%)
GIVETH A NUMBER
`s 211 -- the most interest up to judgment` MEANS
      `simple interest on` `the main part` `at` `rate percent a year` `for` (`the days from` `cause of action arose on` `to` `judgment entered on`)
    + `simple interest on` `non-interest-bearing liabilities paid` `at` `rate percent a year` `for` `the days since paid`
    WHERE
        `the main part` MEANS
            `the sum for which judgment is given` - `future loss` - `exemplary or punitive damages` - `non-interest-bearing liabilities not yet paid` - `non-interest-bearing liabilities paid`
        `the days since paid` MEANS
            IF `non-interest-bearing liabilities paid` GREATER THAN 0
            THEN max 0 (`the days from` `liabilities paid on` `to` `judgment entered on`)
            ELSE 0

-- s 212(1): "This section does not apply to family law or child support proceedings." (2): "A judgment debt ... carries
-- interest from the date as of which the judgment is entered." (3): "Interest is payable: (a) at such rate as is fixed by
-- the Rules of Court; or (b) if the ... Court, in a particular case, thinks that justice so requires--at such lower rate
-- as the Court determines." The Rules are not deposited: the rate is an input. The Act says neither simple nor compound;
-- simple interest at 365 days a year is this encoding's assumption (fork F5).
@export How much interest does my judgment debt carry from the day judgment is entered until it is paid (Federal Circuit and Family Court of Australia Act 2021 (Cth) s 212)? A judgment debt carries interest from the date the judgment is entered, at the rate fixed by the Rules of Court, or a lower rate the Court sets where justice requires. Not in family law or child support proceedings. Calculated as simple interest at 365 days a year.
GIVEN `my kind of proceeding` IS A `My kind of proceeding` @desc What your proceeding is about
      `the judgment debt` IS A NUMBER @desc The judgment debt, in dollars
      `judgment entered on` IS A DATE @desc The day as of which the judgment was entered
      `paid on` IS A DATE @desc The day the debt is (or will be) paid
      `the Rules rate percent a year` IS A NUMBER @desc The interest rate fixed by the Rules of Court, as a percentage a year (the Rules are not deposited here)
      `a lower rate set by the Court` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Court has determined a lower rate for your case because justice so requires
      `the lower rate percent a year` IS A NUMBER @desc That lower rate, as a percentage a year (if none, any number; it is not used)
GIVETH A MAYBE NUMBER
`s 212 -- interest on my judgment debt` MEANS
    IF `s 7 -- a family law or child support proceeding` `my kind of proceeding`
    THEN NOTHING -- the text's no for s 212 ("does not apply"); Family Law Act s 117B governs, which this encoding does not answer
    ELSE JUST (`simple interest on` `the judgment debt` `at` `the rate` `for` (max 0 (`the days from` `judgment entered on` `to` `paid on`)))
    WHERE
        `the rate` MEANS
            IF `a lower rate set by the Court` AND `the lower rate percent a year` LESS THAN `the Rules rate percent a year`
            THEN `the lower rate percent a year`
            ELSE `the Rules rate percent a year`

-- s 213(2): "A person in whose favour a judgment ... is given is entitled to the same remedies for enforcement of the
-- judgment in a State or Territory, by execution or otherwise, as are allowed in like cases by the laws of that State or
-- Territory to persons in whose favour a judgment of the Supreme Court of that State or Territory is given." (3): subject
-- to the Rules. (1): not family law or child support. s 131(3): judgments "have effect and may be executed, throughout
-- Australia". s 173(4): no writ of attachment to enforce payment under an order in the terms of an arbitration award.
@export How can I enforce a judgment I won in the Federal Circuit and Family Court of Australia (Division 2) (Federal Circuit and Family Court of Australia Act 2021 (Cth) ss 131(3), 173(4), 213)? In any State or Territory, with the same remedies (execution or otherwise) as a judgment of that State's or Territory's Supreme Court, subject to the Rules of Court; judgments have effect throughout Australia. A writ of attachment cannot be used to enforce payment under an order made in the terms of an arbitration award. Family law and child support judgments are enforced under the Family Law Act 1975.
GIVEN `my kind of proceeding` IS A `My kind of proceeding` @desc What your proceeding is about
      `an order in the terms of an arbitration award` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the judgment is an order made in the terms of an arbitration award under s 173
GIVETH A MAYBE (LIST OF STRING)
`s 213 -- how I can enforce my judgment` MEANS
    IF `s 7 -- a family law or child support proceeding` `my kind of proceeding`
    THEN NOTHING -- the text's no for s 213 ("does not apply"); the Family Law Act 1975 governs, which this encoding does not answer
    ELSE JUST (LIST
        "the judgment has effect and may be executed throughout Australia (s 131(3))",
        "in each State or Territory you have the same enforcement remedies, by execution or otherwise, as a person with a judgment of that State's or Territory's Supreme Court (s 213(2)), subject to the Rules of Court (s 213(3))",
        `the attachment line`)
    WHERE
        `the attachment line` MEANS
            IF `an order in the terms of an arbitration award`
            THEN "but a writ of attachment must not be issued to enforce payment of money under an order in the terms of an arbitration award (s 173(4))"
            ELSE "other Acts and the Rules of Court may add further means of enforcement (s 213(4))"

§ `Costs and security for costs`

-- s 214(1): "This section does not apply to: (a) family law or child support proceedings; or (b) proceedings in relation
-- to a matter arising under: (ia) Division 2 of Part IIB of the Australian Human Rights Commission Act 1986 (redress for
-- unlawful discrimination); or (i) the Fair Work Act 2009; or (ii) section 14, 15 or 16 of the Public Interest Disclosure
-- Act 2013." (2): the Court "has jurisdiction to award costs in all proceedings before the Court (including proceedings
-- dismissed for want of jurisdiction) other than proceedings in respect of which any other Act provides that costs must
-- not be awarded." (3): "Except as provided by the Rules of Court or any other Act, the award of costs is in the
-- discretion of the" Court.
DECLARE `What my claim arises under` IS ONE OF
    `the Fair Work Act 2009`
    `Division 2 of Part IIB of the Australian Human Rights Commission Act 1986 (an unlawful discrimination claim)`
    `section 14, 15 or 16 of the Public Interest Disclosure Act 2013`
    `another law`

DECLARE `Can costs be awarded under s 214` IS ONE OF
    `yes: costs are in the Court's discretion, even if the proceeding is dismissed for want of jurisdiction (s 214(2), (3))`
    `no: another Act says costs must not be awarded in this proceeding (s 214(2))`
    `s 214 does not apply: see Fair Work Act 2009 s 570 (s 214(1)(b)(i))`
    `s 214 does not apply: see Australian Human Rights Commission Act 1986 s 46PSA (s 214(1)(b)(ia))`
    `s 214 does not apply: see Public Interest Disclosure Act 2013 s 18 (s 214(1)(b)(ii))`
    `s 214 does not apply: family law or child support (see Family Law Act 1975 s 114UB) (s 214(1)(a))`

@export Can I be ordered to pay the other side's costs (or get mine paid) in the Federal Circuit and Family Court of Australia (Division 2), and does this Act decide it (Federal Circuit and Family Court of Australia Act 2021 (Cth) s 214)? For most claims, costs are in the Court's discretion, including when a claim is dismissed for want of jurisdiction, unless another Act says costs must not be awarded. This section does not apply to family law or child support, Fair Work Act, unlawful discrimination (AHRC Act) or public interest disclosure (ss 14-16) claims: their own Acts govern costs.
GIVEN `my kind of proceeding` IS A `My kind of proceeding` @desc What your proceeding is about
      `my claim arises under` IS A `What my claim arises under` @desc The law your claim arises under
      `another Act bars costs` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether another Act provides that costs must not be awarded in your kind of proceeding
GIVETH A `Can costs be awarded under s 214`
`s 214 -- can costs be awarded` MEANS
    IF `s 7 -- a family law or child support proceeding` `my kind of proceeding`
    THEN `s 214 does not apply: family law or child support (see Family Law Act 1975 s 114UB) (s 214(1)(a))`
    ELSE IF `my claim arises under` EQUALS `the Fair Work Act 2009`
         THEN `s 214 does not apply: see Fair Work Act 2009 s 570 (s 214(1)(b)(i))`
         ELSE IF `my claim arises under` EQUALS `Division 2 of Part IIB of the Australian Human Rights Commission Act 1986 (an unlawful discrimination claim)`
              THEN `s 214 does not apply: see Australian Human Rights Commission Act 1986 s 46PSA (s 214(1)(b)(ia))`
              ELSE IF `my claim arises under` EQUALS `section 14, 15 or 16 of the Public Interest Disclosure Act 2013`
                   THEN `s 214 does not apply: see Public Interest Disclosure Act 2013 s 18 (s 214(1)(b)(ii))`
                   ELSE IF `another Act bars costs`
                        THEN `no: another Act says costs must not be awarded in this proceeding (s 214(2))` -- the text's no: "other than"
                        ELSE `yes: costs are in the Court's discretion, even if the proceeding is dismissed for want of jurisdiction (s 214(2), (3))`

-- s 215(2): the Court "may order an applicant ... to give security for the payment of costs that may be awarded against
-- the applicant." (3): "of such amount, and given at such time and in such manner and form, as the ... Court ...
-- directs." (4): the Court may "(a) reduce or increase the amount ...; and (b) vary the time". (5): "If security, or
-- further security, is not given in accordance with an order under this section, the ... Court ... may order that the
-- proceeding be: (a) dismissed; or (b) stayed until security or further security is given". (1): not family law or child
-- support. The duty to give security by the time directed is a regulative rule; days are counted from the order (or the
-- variation), as the direction's own period.
DECLARE `Claimant` IS ONE OF `the applicant`, `the party`
DECLARE `Claim step` IS ONE OF `give the security`, `apply for review of the delegate's decision`

GIVEN `the days allowed` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A DEONTIC `Claimant` `Claim step`
`s 215 -- give the security within the time the Court directs` `the days allowed` MEANS
    PARTY `the applicant`
    MUST `give the security`
    WITHIN `the days allowed`
    HENCE FULFILLED
    LEST BREACH BY `the applicant` BECAUSE "security not given in accordance with the order: the Court may dismiss the proceeding or stay it until security is given (s 215(5))"

-- Runs the rule over what happened. On the stable l4 a CONSIDER on an EVALTRACE matches FULFILLED; anything else is late.
GIVEN `the days allowed` IS A NUMBER
      `the day` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `s 215 -- security given on this day is in time` `the days allowed` `the day` IF
    CONSIDER EVALTRACE (`s 215 -- give the security within the time the Court directs` `the days allowed`) 0 (LIST (WAITUNTIL `the day`), (EVENT `the applicant` `give the security` `the day`))
    WHEN FULFILLED THEN TRUE
    OTHERWISE FALSE

DECLARE `Where my security for costs stands` IS ONE OF
    `given in time: s 215(5) is not engaged`
    `not yet due: give it by the last day`
    `not given in time: the Court may dismiss my proceeding or stay it until security is given (s 215(5))`
    `s 215 does not apply: family law or child support (s 215(1))`

@export I have been ordered to give security for costs: am I in time, and what happens if I do not give it (Federal Circuit and Family Court of Australia Act 2021 (Cth) s 215)? The Court may order an applicant to give security for the costs that may be awarded against them, in the amount, at the time and in the form it directs, and may vary the amount or time. If security is not given as ordered, the Court may dismiss the proceeding or stay it until the security is given. Not in family law or child support proceedings.
GIVEN `my kind of proceeding` IS A `My kind of proceeding` @desc What your proceeding is about
      `ordered on` IS A DATE @desc The day of the order for security (or of the latest order varying the time)
      `the last day directed` IS A DATE @desc The last day the order (as varied) allows for giving the security
      `I have given it` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have given the security in the amount, manner and form ordered
      `given on` IS A DATE @desc The day you gave it (if you have not, any date; it is not used)
      `today` IS A DATE @desc Today's date (used when you have not given it)
GIVETH A `Where my security for costs stands`
`s 215 -- where my security for costs stands` MEANS
    IF `s 7 -- a family law or child support proceeding` `my kind of proceeding`
    THEN `s 215 does not apply: family law or child support (s 215(1))`
    ELSE IF `I have given it`
         THEN IF `s 215 -- security given on this day is in time` `the days allowed` (`the days from` `ordered on` `to` `given on`)
              THEN `given in time: s 215(5) is not engaged`
              ELSE `not given in time: the Court may dismiss my proceeding or stay it until security is given (s 215(5))`
         ELSE IF `s 215 -- security given on this day is in time` `the days allowed` (`the days from` `ordered on` `to` `today`)
              THEN `not yet due: give it by the last day` -- the rule run as if I gave it today
              ELSE `not given in time: the Court may dismiss my proceeding or stay it until security is given (s 215(5))`
    WHERE
        `the days allowed` MEANS `the days from` `ordered on` `to` `the last day directed`

§ `Review of a Registrar's decision`

-- s 256(1): "A party to proceedings in which a delegate has exercised any of the powers of the ... Court ... under
-- section 254 may: (a) within the time prescribed by the Rules of Court; or (b) within any further time allowed in
-- accordance with the Rules of Court; apply to the Court for review of that exercise of power." (2): the Court may review
-- on application "or on its own initiative" and "may make any order or orders it thinks fit". The Rules are not
-- deposited: the days they prescribe are an input. The days are counted from the delegate's decision (fork F6).
GIVEN `the days allowed` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A DEONTIC `Claimant` `Claim step`
`s 256 -- apply for review within the time the Rules prescribe` `the days allowed` MEANS
    PARTY `the party`
    DO `apply for review of the delegate's decision`
    WITHIN `the days allowed`
    HENCE FULFILLED
    LEST BREACH BY `the party` BECAUSE "no application within the time prescribed by the Rules of Court: only within further time allowed under the Rules (s 256(1)(b))"

GIVEN `the days allowed` IS A NUMBER
      `the day` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `s 256 -- an application on this day is in time` `the days allowed` `the day` IF
    CONSIDER EVALTRACE (`s 256 -- apply for review within the time the Rules prescribe` `the days allowed`) 0 (LIST (WAITUNTIL `the day`), (EVENT `the party` `apply for review of the delegate's decision` `the day`))
    WHEN FULFILLED THEN TRUE
    OTHERWISE FALSE

DECLARE `Can I have the delegate's decision reviewed` IS ONE OF
    `yes: I applied (or can apply) in time (s 256(1)(a))`
    `yes: within the further time allowed (s 256(1)(b))`
    `only if further time is allowed under the Rules (s 256(1)(b)); the Court may also review on its own initiative (s 256(2))`

@export Can I ask a Judge to review a decision a Registrar (a delegate) made in my case in the Federal Circuit and Family Court of Australia (Division 2), and am I in time (Federal Circuit and Family Court of Australia Act 2021 (Cth) s 256)? A party may apply for review within the time the Rules of Court prescribe, or any further time allowed under the Rules; the Court may then make any order it thinks fit. Enter the number of days the Rules allow, since the Rules are not part of this encoding.
GIVEN `decided on` IS A DATE @desc The day the delegate (a Registrar) made the decision
      `days the Rules allow` IS A NUMBER @desc The number of days the Rules of Court allow for applying for review, counted from the decision
      `I have applied` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have already applied for review
      `applied on` IS A DATE @desc The day you applied (if you have not, any date; it is not used)
      `today` IS A DATE @desc Today's date (used when you have not yet applied)
      `further time allowed` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether further time to apply has been allowed under the Rules of Court
GIVETH A `Can I have the delegate's decision reviewed`
`s 256 -- can I have the delegate's decision reviewed` MEANS
    IF `s 256 -- an application on this day is in time` `days the Rules allow` `the day`
    THEN `yes: I applied (or can apply) in time (s 256(1)(a))`
    ELSE IF `further time allowed`
         THEN `yes: within the further time allowed (s 256(1)(b))`
         ELSE `only if further time is allowed under the Rules (s 256(1)(b)); the Court may also review on its own initiative (s 256(2))` -- the text's no for (a): a period the text fixes
    WHERE
        `the day` MEANS
            IF `I have applied`
            THEN `the days from` `decided on` `to` `applied on`
            ELSE `the days from` `decided on` `to` `today`

§ `Appeals`

-- s 26(1)(c): Division 1 hears appeals from "a judgment of the Federal Circuit and Family Court of Australia (Division 2)
-- exercising the original jurisdiction mentioned in section 132" (family law and child support); (h): and from a decision
-- "rejecting an application that the Judge ... disqualify himself or herself". (2)(b): "An appeal must not be brought
-- from ... a decision to do, or not to do, any of the following: (i) join or remove a party; (ii) adjourn or expedite a
-- hearing; (iii) vacate a hearing date." s 28(1): leave of Division 1 is required for an appeal from "(a) a judgment of
-- [Division 2] ... exercising original jurisdiction under" the two child support Acts, "(b) a prescribed judgment", "(c)
-- ... rejecting an application that the Judge ... disqualify" in jurisdiction under (a). s 7: "judgment means a
-- judgment, decree or order, whether final or interlocutory, a decision or a sentence", so (b) reaches a prescribed
-- disqualification decision too; (c) adds to (b) and takes nothing from it (fork F8). s 149(4), s 153(6): "An appeal
-- does not lie from a decision ... in relation to the transfer of a proceeding". Appeals from Division 2 in other (general
-- federal law, Fair Work) proceedings lie to the Federal Court under the Federal Court of Australia Act 1976, which this
-- encoding does not answer. s 155: no appeal "directly to the High Court from a judgment of" Division 2.
DECLARE `The Division 2 decision` IS ONE OF
    `a judgment deciding the case, or another order`
    `a decision to join or remove a party`
    `a decision to adjourn or expedite a hearing`
    `a decision to vacate a hearing date`
    `a decision about transferring the proceeding to Division 1 or the Federal Court`
    `a decision rejecting my application that the Judge disqualify themself`

DECLARE `My appeal from Division 2` IS ONE OF
    `yes, to Division 1 (s 26(1)(c) or (h)); no leave needed`
    `yes, to Division 1, but only with the leave of Division 1 (s 28(1))`
    `no appeal from this kind of decision (s 26(2)(b))`
    `no appeal from a transfer decision (s 149(4), s 153(6))`
    `this Act does not give the appeal; see the Federal Court of Australia Act 1976`

@export Can I appeal from a judgment or decision of the Federal Circuit and Family Court of Australia (Division 2), and do I need leave (Federal Circuit and Family Court of Australia Act 2021 (Cth) ss 26, 28, 149(4), 153(6), 155)? In family law and child support cases you appeal to Division 1; you need its leave for child support judgments and prescribed judgments. No appeal lies from decisions to join or remove a party, adjourn or expedite a hearing, vacate a hearing date, or transfer the proceeding. You cannot appeal directly to the High Court. Appeals in other cases go to the Federal Court under its own Act.
GIVEN `my kind of proceeding` IS A `My kind of proceeding` @desc What your proceeding in Division 2 was about
      `the decision` IS A `The Division 2 decision` @desc The kind of judgment or decision you want to appeal from
      `a prescribed judgment` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the judgment or decision you want to appeal from is of a kind prescribed by the regulations as needing leave (the regulations are not deposited here)
GIVETH A `My appeal from Division 2`
`ss 26, 28 -- can I appeal from Division 2` MEANS
    IF `the decision` EQUALS `a decision about transferring the proceeding to Division 1 or the Federal Court`
    THEN `no appeal from a transfer decision (s 149(4), s 153(6))` -- the text's no
    ELSE IF (NOT (`s 7 -- a family law or child support proceeding` `my kind of proceeding`))
         THEN `this Act does not give the appeal; see the Federal Court of Australia Act 1976` -- NOTHING-kind: points outside the sources
         ELSE IF     `the decision` EQUALS `a decision to join or remove a party`
                  OR `the decision` EQUALS `a decision to adjourn or expedite a hearing`
                  OR `the decision` EQUALS `a decision to vacate a hearing date`
              THEN `no appeal from this kind of decision (s 26(2)(b))` -- the text's no
              ELSE IF    `my kind of proceeding` EQUALS `a child support proceeding (under the Child Support (Assessment) Act 1989 or the Child Support (Registration and Collection) Act 1988; s 132(1)(d))`
                      OR `a prescribed judgment` -- s 28(1)(b): any prescribed judgment, and s 7 makes a decision a "judgment"
                   THEN `yes, to Division 1, but only with the leave of Division 1 (s 28(1))`
                   ELSE `yes, to Division 1 (s 26(1)(c) or (h)); no leave needed`

-- s 55(1): no appeal "directly to the High Court from: (a) a judgment of a single Judge of [Division 1] in the exercise of
-- its appellate jurisdiction in relation to a judgment mentioned in paragraph 26(1)(e) [a court of summary jurisdiction];
-- or (b) a judgment of [Division 1] in the exercise of its original jurisdiction." (6): if (1) is inconsistent with
-- Constitution s 73, it reads "except by special leave of the High Court". (2): from other appellate judgments of
-- Division 1, "except by special leave of the High Court". (3): no appeal to the High Court from an appellate judgment
-- that is "(a) a determination of an application of the kind mentioned in subsection 32(2) [extensions of time, leave to
-- amend grounds, stays, security for costs]; or (b) an order under section 38 [stays]; or (c) a decision to ... (i) join
-- or remove a party; (ii) grant leave to defend a proceeding; (iii) reinstate an appeal ...; (iv) extend the time for
-- making an application for leave to appeal; (v) adjourn or expedite a hearing; (vi) vacate a hearing date." s 155:
-- no direct appeal from Division 2 (with the same s 73 proviso, s 155(2)).
DECLARE `The judgment I want to take to the High Court` IS ONE OF
    `a judgment of Division 2`
    `a judgment of Division 1 in its original jurisdiction`
    `a judgment of a single Judge of Division 1 on appeal from a State or Territory court of summary jurisdiction`
    `a judgment of Division 1 on another appeal, deciding the appeal`
    `a procedural decision of Division 1 on an appeal (extension of time, leave to amend grounds, stay, security for costs, joining or removing a party, leave to defend, reinstatement, adjourning, expediting or vacating a hearing)`

DECLARE `Can I go to the High Court` IS ONE OF
    `only by special leave of the High Court (s 55(2))`
    `not directly (s 55(1) or s 155(1)), save by special leave if the bar would be inconsistent with Constitution s 73 (s 55(6), s 155(2))`
    `no appeal to the High Court (s 55(3))`

@export Can I appeal to the High Court from the Federal Circuit and Family Court of Australia (Federal Circuit and Family Court of Australia Act 2021 (Cth) ss 55, 155)? Not directly from Division 2, from Division 1's original jurisdiction, or from a single Judge of Division 1 hearing an appeal from a magistrates' court. From Division 1's other appellate judgments, only with the High Court's special leave; and not at all from procedural decisions on an appeal such as extensions of time, stays or adjournments.
GIVEN `the judgment` IS A `The judgment I want to take to the High Court` @desc The judgment you want to appeal from
GIVETH A `Can I go to the High Court`
`ss 55, 155 -- can I appeal to the High Court` MEANS
    IF `the judgment` EQUALS `a judgment of Division 1 on another appeal, deciding the appeal`
    THEN `only by special leave of the High Court (s 55(2))`
    ELSE IF `the judgment` EQUALS `a procedural decision of Division 1 on an appeal (extension of time, leave to amend grounds, stay, security for costs, joining or removing a party, leave to defend, reinstatement, adjourning, expediting or vacating a hearing)`
         THEN `no appeal to the High Court (s 55(3))` -- the text's no
         ELSE `not directly (s 55(1) or s 155(1)), save by special leave if the bar would be inconsistent with Constitution s 73 (s 55(6), s 155(2))` -- the text's no, with the text's own proviso

§ `Suppression and non-publication orders`

-- s 228: Part 7 "applies to proceedings ... other than proceedings under the Family Law Act 1975". s 229: the Court "must
-- take into account that a primary objective ... is to safeguard the public interest in open justice". s 230(1): the
-- Court may, "by making a suppression order or non-publication order on grounds permitted by this Part, prohibit or
-- restrict the publication or other disclosure of: (a) information tending to reveal the identity of or otherwise
-- concerning any party to or witness in a proceeding ... or any person who is related to or otherwise associated with"
-- them; "or (b) information that relates to a proceeding ... and is: (i) ... evidence or information about evidence; or
-- (ii) ... obtained by the process of discovery; or (iii) ... produced under a subpoena; or (iv) ... lodged with or filed
-- in the Court." s 231(1): grounds: "(a) ... necessary to prevent prejudice to the proper administration of justice; (b)
-- ... necessary to prevent prejudice to the interests of the Commonwealth or a State or Territory in relation to national
-- or international security; (c) ... necessary to protect the safety of any person; (d) ... necessary to avoid causing
-- undue distress or embarrassment to a party to or witness in a criminal proceeding involving an offence of a sexual
-- nature". Whether an order is "necessary" is the Court's judgment: inputs. s 232(1): on its own initiative or on the
-- application of "a party" or another person with "a sufficient interest". s 232(3): "at any time during a proceeding or
-- after a proceeding has concluded". s 234(2): "no longer than is reasonably necessary".
DECLARE `The information` IS ONE OF
    `information that could identify, or is about, a party or witness, or someone related to or associated with them`
    `evidence, or information about evidence`
    `information obtained by discovery`
    `information produced under a subpoena`
    `information lodged with or filed in the Court`
    `other information`

DECLARE `Can the information be suppressed` IS ONE OF
    `the Court may make the order, specifying the ground and limited to what its purpose needs (ss 230, 231, 232(5), 234)`
    `no: no ground the Act permits is made out (ss 230(1), 231(1))`
    `s 230 does not reach this information`
    `Part 7 does not apply: Family Law Act 1975 Part XIA governs (s 228)`

@export Can the Federal Circuit and Family Court of Australia (Division 2) stop my name, or information from my case, being published or disclosed (Federal Circuit and Family Court of Australia Act 2021 (Cth) ss 228-234)? The Court may make a suppression or non-publication order, on its own initiative or on application by a party or someone with a sufficient interest, during or after the case, about information identifying or concerning a party or witness (or people connected to them) or about evidence, discovery, subpoenaed or filed material, but only where the order is necessary to prevent prejudice to the administration of justice or to national or international security, to protect someone's safety, or to avoid undue distress or embarrassment to a party or witness in a sexual offence case. Open justice must be weighed.
GIVEN `my kind of proceeding` IS A `My kind of proceeding` @desc What your proceeding is about
      `the information` IS A `The information` @desc The information you want suppressed or kept from publication
      `necessary for the administration of justice` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Court finds the order necessary to prevent prejudice to the proper administration of justice
      `necessary for national security` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Court finds the order necessary to prevent prejudice to the Commonwealth's, a State's or a Territory's interests in national or international security
      `necessary for someone's safety` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Court finds the order necessary to protect the safety of any person
      `a sexual offence case` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the proceeding is a criminal proceeding involving an offence of a sexual nature (including an act of indecency), and you are a party or witness in it
      `necessary to avoid undue distress` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Court finds the order necessary to avoid causing you undue distress or embarrassment
GIVETH A `Can the information be suppressed`
`ss 230, 231 -- can the information be suppressed` MEANS
    IF `a proceeding under the Family Law Act 1975` `my kind of proceeding`
    THEN `Part 7 does not apply: Family Law Act 1975 Part XIA governs (s 228)` -- the text's no
    ELSE IF `the information` EQUALS `other information`
         THEN `s 230 does not reach this information` -- NOTHING-kind: a grant limited by its own wording
         ELSE IF    `necessary for the administration of justice`
                 OR `necessary for national security`
                 OR `necessary for someone's safety`
                 OR (`a sexual offence case` AND `necessary to avoid undue distress`)
              THEN `the Court may make the order, specifying the ground and limited to what its purpose needs (ss 230, 231, 232(5), 234)`
              ELSE `no: no ground the Act permits is made out (ss 230(1), 231(1))` -- the text's no: "on grounds permitted by this Part" (fork F7)

-- s 236(1): contravening an order under s 230 is an offence. "Penalty: Imprisonment for 12 months, 60 penalty units or
-- both." (2)-(4): it may be punished as an offence or as contempt, but "the offender is not liable to be punished twice".
-- The penalty unit is set by Crimes Act 1914 s 4AA, indexed, and not brought in: the answer stays in penalty units.
@export What is the most I can be punished for breaching a suppression or non-publication order of the Federal Circuit and Family Court of Australia (Division 2) (Federal Circuit and Family Court of Australia Act 2021 (Cth) s 236)? Imprisonment for 12 months, 60 penalty units, or both. It may be punished as an offence or as contempt of court, but not twice for the same act.
GIVEN `I breached an order under s 230` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you did, or failed to do, something that contravened a suppression or non-publication order made under s 230
GIVETH A MAYBE (LIST OF STRING)
`s 236 -- the most I can be punished for breaching a suppression order` MEANS
    IF `I breached an order under s 230`
    THEN JUST (LIST
        "imprisonment for up to 12 months (s 236(1))",
        "up to 60 penalty units (s 236(1))",
        "or both (s 236(1))",
        "punishable as an offence or as a contempt of court, but not twice (s 236(2)-(4))")
    ELSE NOTHING -- NOTHING-kind: s 236 reaches only a contravention

GIVEN `I breached an order under s 230` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A NUMBER
`s 236 -- the most months of imprisonment` `I breached an order under s 230` MEANS
    IF `I breached an order under s 230` THEN 12 ELSE 0

GIVEN `I breached an order under s 230` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A NUMBER
`s 236 -- the most penalty units` `I breached an order under s 230` MEANS
    IF `I breached an order under s 230` THEN 60 ELSE 0

§ `Vexatious proceedings orders` -- a situation: only if someone asks the Court to restrain you, or you are already restrained

-- s 239(1): "This section applies if the ... Court ... is satisfied: (a) a person has frequently instituted or conducted
-- vexatious proceedings in Australian courts or tribunals; or (b) a person, acting in concert with another person who is
-- subject to a vexatious proceedings order or who is covered by paragraph (a), has instituted or conducted a vexatious
-- proceeding in an Australian court or tribunal." (2): the orders: stay or dismiss proceedings already instituted;
-- prohibit instituting proceedings (or of a type); any other appropriate order (note: filing by mail only, security for
-- costs, costs). (3): not proceedings under the Family Law Act 1975. (5): "must not make a vexatious proceedings order in
-- relation to a person without hearing the person or giving the person an opportunity of being heard." (6): orders under
-- (2)(a) or (b) are final. s 237: acting in concert "does not include ... a person who is so acting as a legal
-- practitioner or representative of the other person."
DECLARE `Can a vexatious proceedings order be made against me` IS ONE OF
    `yes: the Court may stay or dismiss my proceedings, bar me from bringing proceedings (or some kinds), or make other orders such as security for costs (s 239(2))`
    `not yet: the Court must first hear me or give me an opportunity to be heard (s 239(5))`
    `no: the Court is not satisfied of either s 239(1) ground`
    `not under s 239(2): Family Law Act 1975 Part XIB governs (s 239(3))`

@export Can the Federal Circuit and Family Court of Australia (Division 2) make a vexatious proceedings order against me (Federal Circuit and Family Court of Australia Act 2021 (Cth) ss 237, 239)? Only if it is satisfied you have frequently brought or conducted vexatious proceedings in Australian courts or tribunals, or you acted in concert with such a person (or a person already under an order) in a vexatious proceeding; acting as someone's lawyer or representative does not count. You must be heard, or given the chance, first. Orders can stay or dismiss your proceedings, bar new ones, or require security for costs.
GIVEN `my kind of proceeding` IS A `My kind of proceeding` @desc What the proceedings the order would cover are about
      `frequently vexatious` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Court is satisfied you have frequently instituted or conducted vexatious proceedings in Australian courts or tribunals
      `in concert in a vexatious proceeding` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Court is satisfied you instituted or conducted a vexatious proceeding acting in concert with a person who is under a vexatious proceedings order or frequently vexatious
      `only as their lawyer or representative` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you acted with that person only as their legal practitioner or representative
      `heard or given the opportunity` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Court has heard you, or given you an opportunity to be heard, on the order
GIVETH A `Can a vexatious proceedings order be made against me`
`s 239 -- can a vexatious proceedings order be made against me` MEANS
    IF `a proceeding under the Family Law Act 1975` `my kind of proceeding`
    THEN `not under s 239(2): Family Law Act 1975 Part XIB governs (s 239(3))` -- the text's no
    ELSE IF `frequently vexatious` OR (`in concert in a vexatious proceeding` AND (NOT `only as their lawyer or representative`))
         THEN IF `heard or given the opportunity`
              THEN `yes: the Court may stay or dismiss my proceedings, bar me from bringing proceedings (or some kinds), or make other orders such as security for costs (s 239(2))`
              ELSE `not yet: the Court must first hear me or give me an opportunity to be heard (s 239(5))` -- the text's no: "must not ... without"
         ELSE `no: the Court is not satisfied of either s 239(1) ground` -- the text's no: "This section applies if"

-- s 241(1): under an order prohibiting a person from instituting proceedings (or of a type), "(a) the person must not
-- institute proceedings ... without the leave of the Court under section 244; and (b) another person must not, acting in
-- concert with the person, institute" them without leave. (2): "If a proceeding is instituted in contravention of
-- subsection (1), the proceeding is stayed." s 242(3): the leave application needs an affidavit listing every earlier
-- leave application and every other proceeding the applicant has instituted, and disclosing all relevant facts. (4): "The
-- applicant must not serve a copy of the application or affidavit on a person unless an order is made under paragraph
-- 244(1)(a)". s 243(1): may dismiss if the affidavit "does not substantially comply"; (2): "must ... dismiss ... if the
-- Court or Judge considers the proceeding is a vexatious proceeding". s 244(1): before granting, the Court "must" order
-- service on the proposed respondent and give them a hearing; (3), (4): "may grant leave only if it is satisfied the
-- proceeding is not a vexatious proceeding", on conditions.
DECLARE `My new proceeding under a vexatious proceedings order` IS ONE OF
    `the order does not cover it: I may institute it`
    `I need the Court's leave first; without it the proceeding is stayed (s 241)`
    `I have leave: I may institute it, on the Court's conditions (s 244(3))`

@export I (or someone I am acting with) am under a vexatious proceedings order in the Federal Circuit and Family Court of Australia (Division 2): can I start a new proceeding (Federal Circuit and Family Court of Australia Act 2021 (Cth) ss 237, 241)? If the order bars proceedings of that kind, you need the Court's leave first; a proceeding brought without leave is stayed. The same applies to anyone acting in concert with the person under the order, other than as their lawyer or representative.
GIVEN `the order covers this kind of proceeding` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the vexatious proceedings order prohibits instituting proceedings of the kind you want to bring
      `I am the person under the order` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are the person the order was made against
      `acting in concert with them` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you would be bringing the proceeding acting in concert with the person under the order
      `only as their lawyer or representative` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you act with them only as their legal practitioner or representative
      `leave granted` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Court has granted leave under s 244 to institute this proceeding
GIVETH A `My new proceeding under a vexatious proceedings order`
`s 241 -- can I start a new proceeding under a vexatious proceedings order` MEANS
    IF     `the order covers this kind of proceeding`
       AND (`I am the person under the order` OR (`acting in concert with them` AND (NOT `only as their lawyer or representative`)))
    THEN IF `leave granted`
         THEN `I have leave: I may institute it, on the Court's conditions (s 244(3))`
         ELSE `I need the Court's leave first; without it the proceeding is stayed (s 241)` -- the text's no: "must not ... without the leave"
    ELSE `the order does not cover it: I may institute it`

DECLARE `My application for leave` IS ONE OF
    `it must be dismissed: the Court considers the proceeding vexatious (s 243(2))`
    `it may be dismissed: my affidavit does not substantially comply with s 242(3) (s 243(1))`
    `it cannot yet be granted: the Court must first order me to serve the proposed respondent and hear them (s 244(1))`
    `it cannot be granted: the Court is not satisfied the proceeding is not vexatious (s 244(4))`
    `it may be granted, on conditions (s 244(3), (4))`

@export Will the Federal Circuit and Family Court of Australia (Division 2) give me leave to start a proceeding while I am under a vexatious proceedings order (Federal Circuit and Family Court of Australia Act 2021 (Cth) ss 242-244)? Your application must come with an affidavit listing your earlier leave applications and every proceeding you have brought, and disclosing all relevant facts. It must be dismissed if the Court considers the proceeding vexatious, and may be dismissed if the affidavit does not substantially comply. Leave can be granted only after the proposed respondent is served and heard, and only if the Court is satisfied the proceeding is not vexatious.
GIVEN `the Court considers it vexatious` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Court or a Judge considers the proceeding you want to bring is a vexatious proceeding
      `affidavit substantially complies` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your affidavit lists all your earlier leave applications and all other proceedings you have brought, and discloses all relevant facts, at least substantially
      `service ordered and respondent heard` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Court has ordered you to serve the person you want to sue (and anyone else it named), and given them the chance to be heard
      `the Court is satisfied it is not vexatious` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Court is satisfied the proceeding is not a vexatious proceeding
GIVETH A `My application for leave`
`ss 243, 244 -- my application for leave` MEANS
    IF `the Court considers it vexatious`
    THEN `it must be dismissed: the Court considers the proceeding vexatious (s 243(2))`
    ELSE IF (NOT `affidavit substantially complies`)
         THEN `it may be dismissed: my affidavit does not substantially comply with s 242(3) (s 243(1))`
         ELSE IF (NOT `service ordered and respondent heard`)
              THEN `it cannot yet be granted: the Court must first order me to serve the proposed respondent and hear them (s 244(1))` -- the text's no: "Before ... it must"
              ELSE IF `the Court is satisfied it is not vexatious`
                   THEN `it may be granted, on conditions (s 244(3), (4))`
                   ELSE `it cannot be granted: the Court is not satisfied the proceeding is not vexatious (s 244(4))` -- the text's no: "only if"

@export May I serve a copy of my application for leave (and its affidavit) on the person I want to sue (Federal Circuit and Family Court of Australia Act 2021 (Cth) s 242(4))? Not unless the Court has ordered service under s 244(1)(a); if it has, you must serve it as the order says.
GIVEN `service ordered under s 244(1)(a)` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Court has made an order under s 244(1)(a) that you serve the application and affidavit
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `s 242(4) -- may I serve my leave application` IF
    `service ordered under s 244(1)(a)` -- otherwise the text's no: "must not serve ... unless"

§ `Family law and child support: where to start`

-- s 50(1): "A person must not institute family law or child support proceedings (other than appellate proceedings) in the
-- Federal Circuit and Family Court of Australia (Division 1)." (2): if they are, "unless the proceedings are transferred
-- to the Federal Court, the proceedings are, by force of this subsection, transferred to [Division 2]; and ... taken to be
-- as valid as they would have been".
DECLARE `Where my family law case starts` IS ONE OF
    `Division 2; it cannot be started in Division 1 (s 50(1))`
    `it was started in Division 1 by mistake: it is transferred to Division 2 by force of s 50(2) and is as valid as if properly started`
    `it was started in Division 1 by mistake and has been transferred to the Federal Court (s 50(2)(a))`
    `s 50 does not reach it: an appeal, or not a family law or child support proceeding`

@export Where do I start a family law or child support case in the Federal Circuit and Family Court, and what happens if I filed it in Division 1 (Federal Circuit and Family Court of Australia Act 2021 (Cth) s 50)? First instance family law and child support cases must not be started in Division 1. If one is, it is transferred to Division 2 by force of the Act (unless transferred to the Federal Court) and is as valid as if started there.
GIVEN `my kind of proceeding` IS A `My kind of proceeding` @desc What your case is about
      `an appeal` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your proceeding is an appeal
      `filed in Division 1` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you filed it in Division 1
      `transferred to the Federal Court` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether it has been transferred to the Federal Court
GIVETH A `Where my family law case starts`
`s 50 -- where my family law case starts` MEANS
    IF `an appeal` OR (NOT (`s 7 -- a family law or child support proceeding` `my kind of proceeding`))
    THEN `s 50 does not reach it: an appeal, or not a family law or child support proceeding`
    ELSE IF `filed in Division 1`
         THEN IF `transferred to the Federal Court`
              THEN `it was started in Division 1 by mistake and has been transferred to the Federal Court (s 50(2)(a))`
              ELSE `it was started in Division 1 by mistake: it is transferred to Division 2 by force of s 50(2) and is as valid as if properly started`
         ELSE `Division 2; it cannot be started in Division 1 (s 50(1))`
